WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.083
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.383 --> 00:00:08.677
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.977 --> 00:00:16.912
Unit twenty-three: Digital Storytelling. Today you will describe how technology enables readers with causative 'let / allow / enable'.

4
00:00:17.212 --> 00:00:19.736
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.666
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.517 --> 00:00:25.402
Digital.

7
00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:29.032
Digital means using computer technology.

8
00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:32.255
Digital stories can include sound and video.

9
00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.560
Your turn. Say: Digital.

10
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:39.285
Digital.

11
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.051
Narrative.

12
00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.711
Narrative means a story or account of events.

13
00:00:45.317 --> 00:00:48.187
The narrative unfolds across several apps.

14
00:00:48.783 --> 00:00:51.714
Your turn. Say: Narrative.

15
00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:55.637
Narrative.

16
00:00:56.383 --> 00:00:57.535
Interactive.

17
00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:02.779
Interactive means allowing two-way involvement between user and system.

18
00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:06.486
Interactive fiction asks readers to make choices.

19
00:01:07.083 --> 00:01:10.061
Your turn. Say: Interactive.

20
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.087
Interactive.

21
00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:15.758
Platform.

22
00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:19.876
Platform means a digital service where content is shared.

23
00:01:20.483 --> 00:01:22.947
Each platform favours a different story length.

24
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:26.191
Your turn. Say: Platform.

25
00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:30.034
Platform.

26
00:01:30.783 --> 00:01:31.970
Multimedia.

27
00:01:32.983 --> 00:01:36.585
Multimedia means combining text, image, sound and video.

28
00:01:37.183 --> 00:01:39.868
The museum created a multimedia exhibition.

29
00:01:40.483 --> 00:01:43.391
Your turn. Say: Multimedia.

30
00:01:46.233 --> 00:01:47.374
Multimedia.

31
00:01:48.117 --> 00:01:48.920
User.

32
00:01:49.917 --> 00:01:53.286
User means a person who uses a digital product.

33
00:01:53.883 --> 00:01:56.150
The user decides which path to follow.

34
00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:59.402
Your turn. Say: User.

35
00:02:02.267 --> 00:02:03.117
User.

36
00:02:03.883 --> 00:02:04.710
Branching.

37
00:02:05.717 --> 00:02:08.715
Branching means splitting into different possible paths.

38
00:02:09.317 --> 00:02:11.630
Branching stories have several endings.

39
00:02:12.217 --> 00:02:14.683
Your turn. Say: Branching.

40
00:02:17.533 --> 00:02:18.476
Branching.

41
00:02:19.217 --> 00:02:20.195
Immersion.

42
00:02:21.183 --> 00:02:25.122
Immersion means the feeling of being completely absorbed in an experience.

43
00:02:25.717 --> 00:02:27.890
Virtual reality deepens immersion.

44
00:02:28.483 --> 00:02:31.194
Your turn. Say: Immersion.

45
00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:34.988
Immersion.

46
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.904
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:46.142
Visal, your group created a branching story about life in Phnom Penh. How does it work?

48
00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:55.452
Branching stories let readers choose the ending. At each key moment, the app allows users to decide what the character does next.

49
00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.793
Did that change how people engaged with it?

50
00:02:59.883 --> 00:03:08.445
Hugely. Making readers responsible for choices encourages them to replay the story and compare outcomes.

51
00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:11.155
Are there any drawbacks?

52
00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:21.683
Yes. More choice doesn't always enable deeper meaning; it can make the narrative feel scattered, so we limited it to three paths.

53
00:03:22.833 --> 00:03:24.427
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:25.283 --> 00:03:28.920
Grammar focus: Causative structures (let and allow).

55
00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:30.500
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:31.183 --> 00:03:33.938
Branching stories let readers choose the ending.

57
00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:37.627
The app allows users to decide what happens next.

58
00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:41.758
Multimedia enables writers to combine sound and image.

59
00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:55.235
'Let' and 'make' + object + base verb (let readers choose). 'Allow / enable / encourage / force' + object + to-infinitive (allow users to decide). 'Have' + object + base verb is used for arranging services.

60
00:03:55.983 --> 00:04:00.168
Be careful! Don't say: Branching stories let readers to choose.

61
00:04:02.133 --> 00:04:04.795
Say: Branching stories let readers choose.

62
00:04:05.095 --> 00:04:07.768
'Let' + object + base verb (no 'to').

63
00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:11.184
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.872
Stories Without a Final Page.

65
00:04:14.172 --> 00:04:20.409
Digital technology has transformed not only how stories are distributed but also how they are told.

66
00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:26.929
Interactive narratives allow readers to influence the plot, turning them from audience into co-author.

67
00:04:27.933 --> 00:04:33.869
Multimedia platforms enable writers to combine text with sound, video and even location data.

68
00:04:34.867 --> 00:04:39.537
However, the freedom that branching structures offer can weaken the emotional shape of a narrative.

69
00:04:40.533 --> 00:04:46.271
The most successful digital stories therefore let users explore while still guiding them toward meaningful moments.

70
00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:51.032
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:51.883 --> 00:04:54.301
The game lets players blank their own path.

72
00:04:54.601 --> 00:04:58.208
A, to choose. B, choose. C, choosing.

73
00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:03.998
The answer is B: choose.

74
00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:07.974
The platform allows writers blank sound and video.

75
00:05:08.274 --> 00:05:12.287
A, to combine. B, combine. C, combining to.

76
00:05:15.833 --> 00:05:17.984
The answer is A: to combine.

77
00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:22.694
The feeling of being completely absorbed in an experience is blank.

78
00:05:22.994 --> 00:05:26.717
A, branching. B, immersion. C, platform.

79
00:05:30.267 --> 00:05:32.406
The answer is B: immersion.

80
00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:35.550
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:40.327
A, make a choice. B, do a choice. C, take a choose.

82
00:05:43.867 --> 00:05:46.354
The answer is A: make a choice.

83
00:05:47.517 --> 00:05:50.677
According to the reading, what can branching structures weaken?

84
00:05:50.977 --> 00:05:56.500
A, the platform. B, the emotional shape of a narrative. C, multimedia.

85
00:06:00.033 --> 00:06:03.612
The answer is B: the emotional shape of a narrative.

86
00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.137
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:08.983 --> 00:06:11.935
How do interactive stories change the role of the reader?

88
00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:30.227
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:30.527 --> 00:07:03.350
Interactive stories change the reader from a passive receiver into an active participant. When a story lets readers make decisions, they feel responsible for the outcome, which can deepen their emotional involvement. For example, in a game about migration, choosing what to leave behind made me understand refugees' difficult choices. Some critics argue that too much freedom destroys the author's vision. I partly agree, but well-designed stories allow freedom while still guiding the reader.

90
00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:05.429
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:06.317 --> 00:07:11.140
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and causative structures (let and allow).

92
00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:15.428
Today's value is creativity. Use your imagination when you play.

93
00:07:15.728 --> 00:07:18.624
See you in Unit twenty-four. Goodbye!

94
00:07:18.924 --> 00:07:22.331
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

